    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I am happy to provide you with a recipe for Tong Sui. Tong Sui is a traditional Hong Kong dessert that is typically made with a sweet, syrupy base and is often served chilled. Here is a simple recipe for you to try:\n\nIngredients:\n- 1 cup of brown sugar\n- 2 cups of water\n- 1/2 cup of glutinous rice flour\n- 1/4 cup of water\n- 1/2 cup of frozen mango chunks\n- 1/2 cup of frozen strawberries\n- 1/2 cup of frozen lychees\n- 1/4 cup of frozen pineapple chunks\n- 1/4 cup of frozen dragon fruit chunks\n- 1/2 cup of coconut milk\n- 1/4 cup of chopped roasted peanuts\n- 1/4 cup of toasted coconut flakes\n- 1/4 cup of crushed ice\n\nInstructions:\n1. In a large saucepan, combine the brown sugar and water and bring to a boil over medium heat.\n2. In a small bowl, whisk together the glutinous rice flour and 1/4 cup of water until smooth.\n3. Gradually pour the flour mixture into the boiling sugar mixture, whisking constantly to prevent lumps.\n4. Continue to cook the mixture, stirring constantly, until it thickens and becomes syrupy.\n5. Add the mango, strawberries, lychees, pineapple, and dragon fruit to the syrup and stir to combine.\n6. Add the coconut milk and stir until well blended.\n7. Remove the saucepan from the heat and let the mixture cool slightly.\n8. Pour the mixture into a serving bowl and refrigerate until chilled.\n9. Just before serving, top the Tong Sui with the chopped peanuts, toasted coconut flakes, and crushed ice.\n10. Serve the Tong Sui chilled and enjoy!\n\nI hope you enjoy this recipe for Tong Sui. Let me know if you have any questions or if you would like me to recommend any other Hong Kong dishes.": -6.1875,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I am happy to help you prepare a recipe for Tong Sui, a traditional Hong Kong dessert. Here is a simple recipe for you to try at home:\n\nIngredients:\n\n- 1 cup of water\n- 1 cup of sugar\n- 2 tablespoons of cornstarch\n- 1/4 teaspoon of salt\n- 1 cup of red beans, soaked and cooked\n- 1/4 cup of crushed ice\n\nInstructions:\n\n1. In a saucepan, combine the water, sugar, and salt, and bring to a boil over medium heat. Stir until the sugar is completely dissolved.\n\n2. In a small bowl, mix the cornstarch with a little water to make a slurry. Gradually add the slurry to the boiling sugar mixture, stirring constantly. Continue to cook until the mixture thickens, about 2-3 minutes.\n\n3. Add the cooked red beans to the saucepan and stir well. Cook for another minute or two until the beans are heated through.\n\n4. Remove from heat and transfer to a serving bowl. Chill in the refrigerator for at least 30 minutes.\n\n5. Before serving, add the crushed ice to the bowl and stir well.\n\n6. Serve cold and enjoy your homemade Tong Sui!": -5.9375,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Tong Sui is a traditional dessert in Hong Kong. It is made with ingredients like sweet potato, red bean, and rice. To make it, first, you will need to cook the sweet potato and rice in water until they are soft. Then, you will need to add the red bean paste to the mixture and stir until it is well combined. Finally, you will need to add sugar to taste and stir until the sugar is dissolved. Serve the Tong Sui warm or cold.": -7.90625,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I'd be happy to help you with that. Tong Sui is a traditional dessert in Hong Kong that is perfect for entertaining guests. Here's a simple recipe for you:\n\nIngredients:\n- 1 cup white sugar\n- 1 cup water\n- 1 cup brown sugar\n- 1 cup water\n- 1/2 cup glutinous rice\n- 1/2 cup black sesame seeds\n- 1/2 cup white sesame seeds\n- 1/2 cup water\n- 1/2 cup pandan leaves\n- 1/2 cup coconut milk\n- 1/4 cup cornstarch\n- 1/4 cup water\n\nInstructions:\n1. In a large pot, combine the white sugar and water and bring to a boil.\n2. Stir in the brown sugar and water, and continue to boil until the sugar is fully dissolved.\n3. Add the glutinous rice, black sesame seeds, and white sesame seeds to the pot and cook for 10-15 minutes, or until the rice is soft.\n4. In a separate pot, combine the pandan leaves, coconut milk, and water, and bring to a boil.\n5. Stir in the cornstarch and water mixture and cook for another 2-3 minutes.\n6. Pour the coconut milk mixture into the pot with the sesame seeds and rice, and stir well.\n7. Let the Tong Sui cool slightly before serving.": -7.4375,
