    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Sure, I can do that. Here is a recipe for a popular dessert called Tong Sui.\n\nIngredients:\n\n- 2 cups of water\n- 1 cup of sugar\n- 2 cups of black beans, soaked overnight\n- 1/4 cup of cornstarch\n- 1 teaspoon of vanilla extract\n\nInstructions:\n\n1. In a large pot, combine the water and sugar and bring to a boil over medium heat.\n2. Add the black beans and vanilla extract and cook for 20 minutes, or until the beans are soft.\n3. In a small bowl, whisk together the cornstarch and a small amount of water to make a slurry.\n4. Add the slurry to the pot and stir until the mixture thickens.\n5. Serve the Tong Sui warm or chilled, garnished with a sprinkle of cinnamon or a drizzle of coconut milk.": -7.71875,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Sure, I'd be happy to share a recipe for Tong Sui, a traditional dessert from Hong Kong. This recipe is for Sweet Black Sesame Tong Sui.": -7.6875,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Sure, I'd be happy to help you with that. Here's a simple recipe for Tong Sui, a traditional Hong Kong dessert:\n\nIngredients:\n- 2 cups of water\n- 1/2 cup of sugar\n- 1/2 cup of glutinous rice\n- 1/4 cup of black sesame seeds\n- 1/4 cup of white sesame seeds\n- 1/2 teaspoon of salt\n\nInstructions:\n1. Rinse the glutinous rice under cold water and drain.\n2. In a saucepan, combine the water, sugar, and salt. Bring to a boil over medium heat, stirring until the sugar is dissolved.\n3. Add the black and white sesame seeds to the boiling water and stir.\n4. Add the glutinous rice to the saucepan and reduce the heat to low. Stir occasionally and cook for about 30 minutes, or until the rice is cooked and the mixture thickens.\n5. Remove from heat and let it cool for a few minutes.\n6. Serve warm or chilled, garnished with additional sesame seeds if desired.\n\nEnjoy your homemade Tong Sui!": -6.53125,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I'd be happy to help you with that! Here's a recipe for Tong Sui, a traditional Hong Kong dessert:\n\nIngredients:\n- 1 cup of white sugar\n- 1/2 cup of water\n- 1/2 cup of sweetened condensed milk\n- 1/4 cup of black sesame seeds\n- 1/4 cup of glutinous rice flour\n- 1/4 cup of water\n- 1/2 teaspoon of vanilla extract\n- 1/4 teaspoon of salt\n- 1/4 cup of chopped nuts (optional)\n\nInstructions:\n1. In a small saucepan, combine the sugar and water and bring to a boil over medium heat. Stir until the sugar is completely dissolved.\n2. Add the sweetened condensed milk and black sesame seeds to the saucepan and stir well. Cook over low heat for about 5 minutes, or until the mixture thickens slightly.\n3. In a separate small saucepan, combine the glutinous rice flour and water and stir until smooth. Cook over low heat for about 2 minutes, or until the mixture thickens.\n4. Add the glutinous rice flour mixture to the saucepan with the sugar, sweetened condensed milk, and black sesame seeds and stir well. Cook over low heat for about 5 minutes, or until the mixture thickens further.\n5. Remove the saucepan from the heat and stir in the vanilla extract and salt. Pour the mixture into a serving bowl and let it cool slightly.\n6. If desired, garnish the Tong Sui with chopped nuts before serving.\n\nEnjoy your homemade Tong Sui!": -7.34375,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Sure, I can help you with that. I will first give you a recipe for Tong Sui, also known as sweet soup, which is a popular dessert in Hong Kong. The ingredients you will need are 1/2 cup of sugar, 2 cups of water, 1/4 cup of cornstarch, and 1 tablespoon of vanilla extract. The instructions are as follows: In a saucepan, combine sugar and water and bring to a boil over medium heat, stirring until sugar is dissolved. In a small bowl, whisk together cornstarch and a small amount of water until smooth. Gradually add cornstarch mixture to saucepan, whisking constantly. Cook for 1-2 minutes or until thickened. Remove from heat and stir in vanilla extract. Pour into individual bowls and serve warm or chilled.": -7.625,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Of course! Here is a recipe for Tong Sui: \n\nIngredients: \n1 can (13.5 oz) of coconut milk \n1 1/2 cups of water \n1/4 cup of granulated sugar \n1/4 cup of white rice, rinsed and drained \n1/2 cup of ice cubes \n1/4 teaspoon of salt \nInstructions: \n1. In a medium saucepan, combine the coconut milk, water, sugar, rice, and salt. \n2. Bring the mixture to a boil over medium heat, stirring occasionally. \n3. Once the mixture has reached a boil, reduce the heat to low and simmer for 15-20 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the rice is fully cooked and the mixture has thickened. \n4. Remove the saucepan from the heat and stir in the ice cubes until they have melted. \n5. Taste the Tong Sui and adjust the sweetness if necessary by adding more sugar. \n6. Serve the Tong Sui hot or cold, depending on your preference. \n\nEnjoy!": -8.5,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Tong Sui is a sweet dessert soup, and it can be made with a variety of ingredients. One simple recipe is to combine 1 cup of water, 1/2 cup of sugar, and a few slices of fresh ginger in a pot. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce the heat and let it simmer for about 10 minutes. You can also add ingredients like cubed fruit, tapioca pearls, or coconut milk to make the soup thicker and creamier. Remember to taste and adjust the sweetness and spices according to your preference. Enjoy your Hong Kong-style Tong Sui!": -6.875,
